CUH='https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/patient-information-for-patients-undergoing-treatment-for-a-deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/'
UCLH='https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/deep-vein-thrombosis'
D['deep-vein-thrombosis']=dict(searches=5,secs=[
("How is a DVT assessed in practice?",[N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/',CUH],
"The NHS says that if a doctor thinks someone has a DVT, they should be referred to hospital within 24 hours for an ultrasound scan. The scan shows whether blood is flowing normally through the vein.\n\nA hospital leaflet describes how the D-dimer blood test fits in. The doctor first estimates the likelihood of a clot using a points score called the Wells score. A low score combined with a negative D-dimer is enough to rule out a DVT, and no further tests are needed. If the D-dimer is high, a scan is needed. If the Wells score is moderate or high, the D-dimer is skipped and the person goes straight to a scan.\n\nDVT can occur in an arm as well as a leg. Common features are pain or tenderness, swelling, warmth, redness or discolouration and swollen surface veins, although about half of people with the condition have no symptoms. The NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E if leg symptoms come with breathlessness or chest pain."),
("How is a DVT treated?",[N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/',CUH,'https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/blood-and-lymph/deep-vein-thrombosis/'],
"Blood-thinning medicines (anticoagulants) are the main treatment. Current NHS advice favours tablets such as rivaroxaban or apixaban, although warfarin is also used. The NHS says people usually need to take them for at least three months to reduce the risk of another clot.\n\nA Cambridge hospital leaflet gives more detail on length. For a first DVT with no clear cause, three to six months of treatment is usual, depending on the bleeding risk. For someone who has had a DVT before, staying on treatment for life is normally recommended.\n\nBlood thinners do not dissolve the clot. The body absorbs it over time. The medicine stops it growing and helps prevent new clots. Clot-busting drugs or surgery are reserved for severe cases."),
("Living with blood thinners",[CUH,'https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/information-for-patients-following-an-episode-of-deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt-or-pulmonary-embolism-pe/'],
"All anticoagulants increase the risk of bleeding. A Cambridge hospital leaflet lists the signs that need prompt advice from a GP, NHS 111 or, in an emergency, 999: black or bloody stools, blood in the urine, coughing or vomiting blood, severe period bleeding, heavy or persistent nosebleeds and unexplained severe bruising.\n\nThe same leaflet says aspirin and anti-inflammatory painkillers such as ibuprofen, naproxen or diclofenac should be avoided unless a GP advises otherwise, and that paracetamol is the painkiller generally used alongside anticoagulants. It also advises checking with a doctor before taking hormone replacement therapy or the combined contraceptive pill after a clot.\n\nOn travel, a Cambridge leaflet advises delaying flights or long journeys until at least two weeks after starting blood-thinning medicine. It adds that aspirin has not been shown to reduce the risk of clots on long journeys, and that anyone who develops leg pain or swelling, chest pain or breathlessness afterwards should seek medical advice."),
("What are the long-term effects of a DVT?",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/blood-and-lymph/deep-vein-thrombosis/',UCLH,'https://www.nth.nhs.uk/resources/deep-vein-thrombosis/'],
"The most serious complication is a pulmonary embolism, in which a clot travels to the lungs. It can be life-threatening and needs to be treated quickly.\n\nPost-thrombotic syndrome is the main long-term problem in the leg. Hospital leaflets describe chronic leg pain, swelling, redness and sometimes sores or ulcers. It usually develops within the first two years. Estimates of how many people are affected vary between sources, and it is more likely after extensive clots.\n\nCompression stockings are a common way to manage the symptoms. A UCLH leaflet says they should not be worn until the initial leg swelling has settled, and another hospital leaflet notes that people with circulation or skin problems may not be able to use them. Some people who develop post-thrombotic syndrome are advised to wear them for more than two years.\n\nThe NHS advises avoiding long periods of sitting still, and walking around regularly or doing calf exercises."),
("Who is at risk of a DVT, and what about pregnancy?",[N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/',CUH,'https://www.swbh.nhs.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Maternity-Deep-Vein-Thrombosis-in-pregnancy-ML6332.pdf'],
"A Cambridge hospital leaflet lists these risk factors: a clotting abnormality in the blood, including a family history, pregnancy, obesity, an underlying cancer, a previous DVT, immobility including long-haul flights, contraceptive pills, recent surgery and intravenous drug use. The NHS adds smoking, being over 60, taking hormone replacement therapy and having varicose veins.\n\nA Black Country hospital leaflet says about one in 1,000 pregnant women have a DVT while pregnant or within about six weeks of giving birth. The same leaflet says warfarin is known to be harmful to a developing baby and that the effects of the newer direct oral anticoagulants in pregnancy are unknown, so anyone who is or may become pregnant should tell their doctor straight away so that a different anticoagulant can be prescribed. Injections of low-molecular-weight heparin are used instead.\n\nIn hospital, patients are assessed for their clot risk and may be given compression stockings or preventive medicine.")
],faqs=[
("Does a blood thinner dissolve the clot?","No. A hospital leaflet says the blood thinner does not dissolve the clot and the body absorbs it over time. The medicine stops the clot growing and helps prevent new ones forming. Clot-busting drugs or surgery are kept for severe cases.",[CUH,N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/']),
("How long are blood thinners taken for?","The NHS says people usually need them for at least three months. A Cambridge leaflet says a first DVT with no clear cause is usually treated for three to six months, while a repeat DVT normally means treatment for life. The clinician sets the length.",[N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/',CUH]),
("Can I fly after a DVT?","A Cambridge hospital leaflet advises delaying flights or long journeys until at least two weeks after starting blood-thinning medicine. Anyone who develops leg pain or swelling, chest pain or breathlessness after a long journey should seek medical advice.",[CUH]),
("Can ibuprofen be taken with anticoagulants?","A Cambridge hospital leaflet says aspirin and anti-inflammatory medicines such as ibuprofen, naproxen or diclofenac should be avoided unless a GP advises otherwise, and that paracetamol is generally used instead. Anyone unsure should ask a pharmacist or GP.",[CUH]),
("When does a DVT become an emergency?","The NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E if leg symptoms come with breathlessness or chest pain, as this can mean the clot has reached the lungs. A swollen, painful leg alone needs a same-day medical assessment, with a scan within 24 hours.",[N+'deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/']),
],facts=[
("What it is","A blood clot in a deep vein, most often in the leg"),
("Emergency route","Call 999 or go to A&E if leg symptoms come with breathlessness or chest pain"),
("Scan","Ultrasound, usually within 24 hours of a doctor suspecting DVT"),
("Usual treatment","Anticoagulant (blood-thinning) medicine for at least three months"),
("Main long-term problem","Post-thrombotic syndrome: leg pain, swelling, redness or sores"),
("Serious complication","Pulmonary embolism, where a clot reaches the lungs"),
],refs=[("Cambridge University Hospitals: Treatment for a deep vein thrombosis","https://www.cuh.nhs.uk/patient-information/patient-information-for-patients-undergoing-treatment-for-a-deep-vein-thrombosis-dvt/"),("UCLH: Deep vein thrombosis","https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/deep-vein-thrombosis"),("North Tees and Hartlepool NHS: Deep vein thrombosis","https://www.nth.nhs.uk/resources/deep-vein-thrombosis/")])

PE='https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/pulmonary-embolism/'
D['pulmonary-embolism']=dict(searches=4,secs=[
("What are the symptoms of a pulmonary embolism?",[PE,'https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/pulmonary-embolism-pe'],
"The NHS lists sudden breathlessness, chest pain that gets worse when breathing in and coughing up blood as the main symptoms, with leg pain or swelling also possible if a DVT is present. The NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E for sudden difficulty breathing, chest or upper back pain, a very fast heartbeat or passing out.\n\nSymptoms are not the same for everyone. Hospital leaflets note that some people have no symptoms at all, and that symptoms can also build up slowly over days to weeks rather than suddenly. For this reason a pulmonary embolism cannot be confirmed or ruled out from symptoms alone, and tests are needed."),
("What causes a pulmonary embolism?",['https://www.worcsacute.nhs.uk/leaflets/pulmonary-embolus-diagnosed-waht-pi-2173/','https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/pulmonary-embolism','https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pulmonary-embolism'],
"NHS hospital leaflets say that in almost all cases the cause is a blood clot that formed in a deep vein, known as a DVT, usually in the leg or pelvis, and then travelled to the lungs. A few come from an arm vein or from a clot in the heart.\n\nThe leaflets list similar risk factors. These include immobility, recent surgery or trauma and being in hospital; pregnancy and the period after delivery; oestrogen-containing contraceptives or hormone replacement therapy; cancer, obesity, smoking and older age; and a previous clot or a family history of clots. Some people have an inherited or acquired tendency for their blood to clot (thrombophilia).\n\nSometimes a pulmonary embolism happens for no obvious reason. Doctors describe these as unprovoked, and the likely cause is important in deciding how long treatment continues."),
("How is a pulmonary embolism diagnosed?",['https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/m/2716/003-pulmonary-embolism.pdf','https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/document.ashx?id=6080','https://www.gloshospitals.nhs.uk/your-visit/patient-information-leaflets/pulmonary-embolism/'],
"A Scottish NHS guideline describes a pathway that starts with a clinical probability score of low, intermediate or high, followed by a D-dimer blood test. The Whittington Health guideline says D-dimer is used to exclude the diagnosis in people at low risk, and that imaging is used to confirm it.\n\nThe usual confirming scan is a CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA), which also shows the state of the blood vessels in the lungs. A Gloucestershire hospital leaflet notes that the scan is rarely done on the first visit, so patients usually return for it. D-dimer is not infallible and can miss a clot.\n\nThe Scottish guideline says that while the diagnosis is unconfirmed, a full-strength dose of injected low-molecular-weight heparin or a tablet anticoagulant can be given. Treatment can therefore begin before the scan result is back."),
("How is a pulmonary embolism treated?",[PE,'https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/pulmonary-embolism-pe','https://www.nth.nhs.uk/resources/pulmonary-embolism/'],
"Once the diagnosis is confirmed, treatment usually continues with anticoagulant tablets or injections, depending on what suits the person. Anticoagulants do not dissolve the clot. The body clears it over time. They stop existing clots growing and help prevent new ones forming. Clot-busting drugs are reserved for severe cases.\n\nHospital leaflets say treatment can last anywhere from three months to the rest of a person's life, depending on the cause and the person's risk of another clot. The Whittington Health guideline says the likely cause of the clot is important for deciding how long to treat.\n\nAnticoagulants raise the risk of bleeding. Anyone taking them who has black or bloody stools, vomits or coughs up blood, or has heavy or persistent bleeding should seek urgent medical advice from NHS 111, or call 999 in an emergency."),
("What is recovery from a pulmonary embolism like?",[PE,'https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/pulmonary-embolism-pe'],
"The NHS says a full recovery is expected if a pulmonary embolism is spotted and treated early. Hospital leaflets say breathlessness and tiredness are common and improve gradually, and that a return to normal can take several weeks to months. They advise seeing a GP if breathlessness on exertion persists beyond about eight weeks.\n\nSome people have longer-term problems. One NHS leaflet notes that long-term complications, including breathlessness and heart problems, can follow a pulmonary embolism. This is called chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension.\n\nThe outlook is worse when a serious existing illness, such as advanced cancer, contributed to the clot. For most other people, treated promptly, the picture is much better. Questions about long-term treatment, travel or hormone medicines belong with the treating team or GP.")
],faqs=[
("Where do the clots in a pulmonary embolism come from?","NHS hospital leaflets say that in almost all cases the clot formed in a deep vein, usually in the leg or pelvis, and travelled to the lungs. A few come from an arm vein or from the heart.",['https://www.worcsacute.nhs.uk/leaflets/pulmonary-embolus-diagnosed-waht-pi-2173/']),
("Can a pulmonary embolism cause no symptoms?","Yes. Hospital leaflets say some people have no symptoms, and that symptoms can build up slowly over days to weeks. Tests are needed to confirm or rule out the diagnosis.",['https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/pulmonary-embolism']),
("Why is a D-dimer test not enough on its own?","A D-dimer is used to exclude a pulmonary embolism in people at low risk, not to confirm it. Imaging, usually a CT pulmonary angiogram, is used to confirm the diagnosis. D-dimer can also occasionally miss a clot.",['https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/document.ashx?id=6080','https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/m/2716/003-pulmonary-embolism.pdf']),
("How long does treatment last?","Hospital leaflets say it can last from three months to the rest of a person's life. The length depends on the cause of the clot and the risk of another one, and the treating team makes the decision.",['https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/pulmonary-embolism-pe']),
("How long does recovery take?","Breathlessness and tiredness are common and improve gradually. The NHS says people should make a full recovery if the condition is treated early, though a return to normal can take several weeks to months.",[PE]),
],facts=[
("What it is","A blood clot that blocks an artery in the lung"),
("Emergency route","Call 999 or go to A&E for sudden breathlessness, chest pain, coughing blood or collapse"),
("Usual source","A DVT, most often in the leg or pelvis"),
("Confirming scan","CT pulmonary angiogram (CTPA)"),
("Treatment","Anticoagulant tablets or injections; clot-busting drugs only in severe cases"),
("Length of treatment","From three months to lifelong, depending on cause and risk of recurrence"),
],refs=[("UCLH: Pulmonary embolism","https://www.uclh.nhs.uk/patients-and-visitors/patient-information-pages/pulmonary-embolism-pe"),("Kingston and Richmond NHS: Pulmonary embolism","https://www.kingstonandrichmond.nhs.uk/patients-and-families/patient-leaflets/pulmonary-embolism"),("Right Decisions (NHS Scotland): Pulmonary embolism, investigation and management","https://www.rightdecisions.scot.nhs.uk/m/2716/003-pulmonary-embolism.pdf")])

T='https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/type-1-diabetes/'
D['type-1-diabetes']=dict(searches=6,secs=[
("What causes type 1 diabetes?",[T,'https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/type-1-diabetes/','https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/what-is-diabetes/type-1-diabetes'],
"Type 1 diabetes is a lifelong condition in which the pancreas produces little or no insulin. It is an autoimmune condition: the immune system damages the insulin-producing cells of the pancreas. Without insulin the body cannot move glucose out of the blood and into cells, so regular insulin treatment is needed to keep glucose levels in a healthy range.\n\nThe NHS notes that the tendency can run in families. Researchers have not pinned down what sets off the autoimmune process, and research reviews have suggested that viral infections and other factors may play a part.\n\nType 1 is a different condition from type 2 diabetes, which accounts for the large majority of diabetes worldwide. The NHS lists the classic early signs as needing the toilet more, being thirsty, feeling tired and losing weight, often remembered as the four Ts. NIDDK notes that some people first find out they have type 1 diabetes when they develop signs of diabetic ketoacidosis."),
("How is type 1 diabetes diagnosed?",[T+'symptoms/','https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/what-is-diabetes/type-1-diabetes'],
"A GP will usually test a urine sample and may check blood glucose, then send the person to hospital for assessment. The NHS says people usually stay in hospital until the blood test results are back, which is generally the same day.\n\nThe NHS says symptoms of type 1 diabetes can appear over a few days or weeks. The other signs it lists are blurred vision, sweet or fruity-smelling breath, slow-healing cuts and frequent infections.\n\nBecause diabetic ketoacidosis can be the first sign, the NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E if symptoms worsen quickly, or if stomach pain, sleepiness or confusion, or fast, deep breathing develop. A same-day GP appointment is the usual route for the earlier warning signs in a child or young person."),
("How is type 1 diabetes treated day to day?",['https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/insulin/insulin-for-type-1-diabetes/',T,'https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/type-1-diabetes/'],
"The NHS says treatment means taking insulin for life and regularly checking blood glucose. Most people take both a long-acting insulin, once or twice a day, and a rapid-acting insulin before meals. This pattern is known as basal-bolus treatment.\n\nInsulin can be given with pens or by a pump, a device worn on the body that delivers small amounts of insulin through the day and night. Pump therapy can be used by adults, teenagers and children with adult supervision.\n\nThe amount of mealtime insulin varies with the blood glucose level before the meal and with the amount of carbohydrate eaten, which is why carbohydrate counting is taught. NHS inform lists the other ongoing tasks: checking glucose, recognising the signs of a hypo, attending check-ups and planning for driving and pregnancy.\n\nOne NHS 111 Wales page stresses that treatment should not be stopped during illness, and that the treatment plan may say whether a temporary increase is needed."),
("What is a hypo?",['https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng17/resources/type-1-diabetes-in-adults-diagnosis-and-management-pdf-564275347909','https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/type-1-diabetes/'],
"A hypo is a lower-than-normal level of glucose in the blood. NICE lists dizziness, tiredness, hunger, shaking and sweating among the symptoms, and notes that some people with long-standing diabetes do not always spot the warning signs.\n\nNHS inform says a hypo happens when injected insulin moves too much glucose out of the bloodstream. Common triggers include too much insulin, a skipped meal, vigorous exercise and drinking alcohol on an empty stomach.\n\nMild hypos are usually treated with something sugary. Severe ones may need an injection of glucagon into a muscle or glucose given into a vein.\n\nNICE says the annual review should check that the person can recognise the symptoms of a hypo."),
("What is diabetic ketoacidosis?",['https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/diabetic-ketoacidosis/','https://www.diabetes.org.uk/about-diabetes/looking-after-diabetes/complications/diabetic-ketoacidosis'],
"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) occurs when a lack of insulin lets harmful substances called ketones build up in the blood. It can be life-threatening and needs urgent treatment in hospital. Diabetes UK lists thirst, passing urine more, stomach pain, sickness, deep breathing, fruity-smelling breath, tiredness or confusion and blurred vision. It says symptoms usually develop over about 24 hours but can be faster.\n\nThe NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E if the symptoms are getting worse quickly, or if there is stomach pain, sleepiness or confusion, or fast, deep breathing. People with type 1 diabetes are taught to test for ketones when they are unwell, and the NHS gives the blood and urine levels at which to ring 999, or at which to contact the diabetes team for advice.\n\nUntreated, DKA can cause vomiting, dehydration, unconsciousness and death."),
("What are the long-term complications and checks?",[T+'complications/','https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/complications-of-type-1-diabetes/','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng17/resources/type-1-diabetes-in-adults-diagnosis-and-management-pdf-564275347909'],
"Over time, high glucose can damage blood vessels and nerves. The NHS describes three main areas. In the eyes, damage to blood vessels can cause sight problems (diabetic retinopathy) and, uncommonly, blindness, so adults and children aged 12 or over are offered eye screening every year. In the feet, nerve damage means small cuts may go unnoticed and can become ulcers, so the NHS advises checking the feet every day and having a foot examination at least once a year. In the kidneys, damage usually causes no symptoms at first, so a test is advised every year.\n\nNICE describes an annual review that covers foot checks, dental checks for gum disease and screening for kidney and nerve problems. Cholesterol and blood pressure are also checked, and blood pressure is checked in children too.")
],faqs=[
("Is type 1 diabetes the same as type 2?","No. Type 1 is an autoimmune condition in which the pancreas makes little or no insulin, so insulin is needed from diagnosis. Type 2 is a different condition and accounts for the great majority of diabetes worldwide.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/type-1-diabetes/']),
("What are the four Ts of type 1 diabetes?","The NHS lists needing the toilet more, thirst, tiredness and weight loss as the core signs, often remembered as the four Ts. Blurred vision, fruity-smelling breath, slow-healing cuts and frequent infections can also occur.",[T+'symptoms/']),
("Why do hypos happen?","NHS inform says a hypo happens when injected insulin moves too much glucose out of the blood. Common triggers include taking too much insulin, skipping a meal, vigorous exercise and drinking alcohol on an empty stomach.",['https://www.nhsinform.scot/illnesses-and-conditions/diabetes/type-1-diabetes/']),
("Should insulin carry on during illness?","One NHS 111 Wales page says it is very important not to stop treatment when ill. The treatment plan may say whether a temporary increase is needed, and the diabetes care team is the place to ask.",['https://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/d/article/diabetes,type1/']),
("When is diabetic ketoacidosis an emergency?","The NHS advises calling 999 or going to A&E if symptoms are getting worse quickly, or if there is stomach pain, sleepiness or confusion, or fast, deep breathing. DKA can be life-threatening and needs urgent hospital treatment.",['https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/diabetic-ketoacidosis/']),
],facts=[
("Cause","Autoimmune damage to the insulin-making cells of the pancreas"),
("Classic early signs","Needing the toilet more, thirst, tiredness and weight loss"),
("Treatment","Insulin for life, by pen or pump, with regular glucose checks"),
("Emergency route","Call 999 or go to A&E if ketoacidosis signs worsen quickly or include sleepiness, confusion or fast, deep breathing"),
("Yearly checks","Eye screening, foot examination and kidney tests, per the NHS"),
("Lasts","Lifelong"),
],refs=[("NHS: Insulin for type 1 diabetes","https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/insulin/insulin-for-type-1-diabetes/"),("NHS: Complications of type 1 diabetes","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/type-1-diabetes/complications/"),("NHS: Diabetic ketoacidosis","https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/diabetic-ketoacidosis/"),("NICE: Type 1 diabetes in adults, information for the public (NG17)","https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng17/resources/type-1-diabetes-in-adults-diagnosis-and-management-pdf-564275347909"),("Diabetes UK: Diabetic ketoacidosis","https://www.diabetes.org.uk/about-diabetes/looking-after-diabetes/complications/diabetic-ketoacidosis")])
